\title{Compact-to-Hausdorff Bijections Are Homeomorphisms}
\taxon{Theorem}
\import{macros}
\p{
    Let #{f:X\to Y} be a continuous bijection. If #{X} is compact and #{Y} is
    Hausdorff, then #{f} is a [homeomorphism](def-005O).
}
\proof{
    Let #{C\subseteq X} be closed. It is compact by
    [compactness of closed subspaces](thm-001I), and its image #{f(C)} is
    compact by [preservation of compactness](thm-001J). Since #{Y} is
    Hausdorff, [compact subsets are closed](thm-001K), so #{f(C)} is closed in
    #{Y}. Thus #{f} is a closed map.

    For the inverse map #{f^{-1}:Y\to X}, the preimage of a closed set
    #{C\subseteq X} is
    ##{
        (f^{-1})^{-1}(C)=f(C),
    }
    which is closed in #{Y}. Hence #{f^{-1}} is continuous, and #{f} is a
    homeomorphism.
}
